    "summary": "Former Somali President Mohamed Abdullahi Farmaajo has sent an open letter to President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud urging inclusive political dialogue and agreement on the electoral process. The letter, dated 24 September 2026, highlights political, security, economic, and social challenges facing Somalia.",
    "content": "Former President of Somalia Mohamed Abdullahi Mohamed (Farmaajo) has publicly called on the current President, Hassan Sheikh Mohamud, to engage in comprehensive political dialogue and reach an agreement on the country's electoral process. In an open letter dated 24 September 2026, Farmaajo addressed the political, security, economic, and social situation in Somalia, noting that disputes between the federal government and some federal member states require immediate resolution.\n\nThe letter comes amid ongoing tensions over the timing and structure of upcoming elections. Somalia has faced prolonged delays in finalizing its electoral model, with disagreements between the federal government and regional administrations over power-sharing and the roles of various stakeholders. Farmaajo, who served as president from 2017 to 2022, has been a vocal critic of the current administration's policies.\n\nFarmaajo's appeal emphasizes the need for a broad-based dialogue that includes all political actors, civil society, and regional leaders. He warned that failure to address these issues could exacerbate instability and undermine progress made in recent years. The former president also stressed the importance of transparency and consensus in the electoral process to ensure credible and peaceful polls.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant as it directly affects the political stability and future governance of Somalia. Many diaspora members are actively engaged in Somali politics, either through remittances, investments, or direct participation in elections. A prolonged political impasse could hinder reconstruction efforts and delay the country's path to democratic consolidation, impacting diaspora-led initiatives and family ties back home.\n\nThe letter has sparked mixed reactions among Somali political observers. Some view it as a constructive step toward national unity, while others see it as a political maneuver ahead of anticipated elections. President Hassan Sheikh's office has not yet issued a formal response. The international community, including the United Nations and African Union, has consistently urged Somali leaders to resolve differences through dialogue.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/09/24/farmaajo-oo-xasan-sheekh-u-baaqay-wada-hadal/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=farmaajo-oo-xasan-sheekh-u-baaqay-wada-hadal",
