    "summary": "Somalia's House of the People has suspended Law LR. 132 and a directive on open competition for civil service hiring, ordering merit-based recruitment.",
    "content": "Somalia's House of the People has suspended Law LR. 132 of 2023 and a directive from the Prime Minister's Office that introduced an open competition process for civil service hiring. The parliamentary decision, dated 23 September 2026, was announced in Mogadishu. The directive, issued on 17 September 2026, had aimed to reform recruitment practices. The suspension effectively halts the implementation of both measures.\n\nThe suspended law, LR. 132, was originally passed on 11 May 2023. The directive from the Prime Minister's Office sought to establish a transparent, merit-based system for hiring and promoting government employees. It required the Civil Service Commission to base recruitment and advancement on open competition, free from bias, discrimination, and abuse of power. The parliamentary move reverses these recent efforts.\n\nThe House of the People's decision also instructs the Civil Service Commission to ensure that hiring and promotions are conducted through an open, fair, and equitable competitive process. This instruction appears to reinforce the principles of the suspended directive, though the legal framework supporting it has been removed. The rationale behind the suspension remains unclear, and no further details were provided in the announcement.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant. Many diaspora members have sought to contribute to rebuilding Somalia's institutions, often eyeing civil service roles. The suspension of a law and directive aimed at curbing nepotism and favoritism could undermine confidence in the transparency of government hiring. Diaspora professionals who have returned or are considering returning may view this as a setback for meritocratic reforms.\n\nThe suspension raises questions about the future of civil service reform in Somalia. It is uncertain whether the parliament will propose alternative legislation or if the directive will be reinstated. The Civil Service Commission now faces the challenge of implementing fair hiring practices without the legal instruments previously in place. The international community, which has supported governance reforms, may monitor the situation closely.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/24/golaha-shacabka-oo-laalay-xeer-lr-132-iyo-qaraarkii-hirgelinta-hanaanka-tartanka-shaqaalaha-dowladda/",
